			"notes": "Lumi 0.2.0 RC2 replaces the Alpha history engine with Lumi V2.\nThese changes cover the updates since [Lumi 0.1.0 Alpha](https://github.com/KristopherZlo/lumi/releases/tag/v0.1.0).\n\n## Before you update\n\n**V2 starts a new Lumi history. It does not import Alpha patch-v9 or snapshot-v8 history.**\n\nBefore you update Lumi, make a backup of your world and its Lumi data.\nOld saved versions and branches do not appear in V2 automatically.\n\n## Installation\n\n- Use Minecraft **1.21.11**, Fabric Loader **0.19.2+**, Fabric API **0.141.3+1.21.11**, and Java **21**.\n- Install `lumi-0.2.0-rc.2.jar`.\n- For a dedicated server, also install Lumi on each client that uses Lumi screens or overlays.\n- Server actions require operator permission.\n- **Lumi no longer requires owo-lib or Cloth Config.** WorldEdit and Axiom remain optional. V2 has no separate FAWE integration.\n\n## History and world safety\n\n- Lumi now stores immutable world objects in hash-verified packs. Atomic references and operation journals protect history during interrupted operations.\n- Each initialized dimension has a protected **Initial** saved version.\n- Save captures the exact world state during a dimension freeze. It then releases the freeze and publishes the saved version.\n- Edits after the capture remain pending for the next Save.\n- Restore checks the persisted blocks, block entities, entities, and affected world data before it reports success.\n- Recovery checks which operation phase completed. It avoids another world apply when the persisted result already matches the requested state.\n- History cleanup preserves data that active Save operations still need. Lumi also retries failed origin captures and flushes pending writes during shutdown.\n- Rename changes the display name without a rewrite of the saved version. Deleted history retains recovery references until cleanup.\n\n## Shared worlds and Restore\n\n- Each dimension has one shared active workspace and branch. Only one history operation can change that dimension at a time.\n- Queued actions check permissions, history references, and scope revisions again when they start.\n- After world changes start, the server owns the operation. It completes or recovers the operation if the client disconnects.\n- Full Restore uses a compact confirmation dialog. Partial Restore and outside-selection Restore use the wooden sword selection.\n- Zone Restore applies the exact zone scope.\n- **Quick Restore now uses `Alt+R` by default.** It restores the active workspace to its current saved version. `Alt+Z` can undo Quick Restore.\n- With the wooden sword equipped, Undo and Redo affect selection history only. Empty selection history cannot trigger a world action.\n- Restore and action capture now handle more lighting and entity cases. Tests cover duplicate entity UUIDs and supported WorldEdit/Axiom edits.\n\n## Compare and highlight\n\n- Compare shows two branch/version columns with separate page controls. It opens the world highlight directly.\n- Green, red, and amber still identify added, removed, and changed blocks. The Action key enables x-ray.\n- Compare prepares geometry outside the render thread. It reuses GPU meshes, removes duplicate edges, and limits the resident cache.\n- Camera checks exclude geometry outside the view. Dense leaf changes retain the individual block grid.\n- **H hides the highlight and keeps the prepared buffers.** The same prepared view can appear again without another rebuild.\n- The HUD shows a preparation percentage and progress bar. The first highlight appears when the visible batches are ready.\n\n## Screens and zones\n\n- **Lumi screens now use fixed GUI scale 2.** Tooltips use the same scale across Minecraft GUI Scale settings and window sizes.\n- Zone Save and Amend use current zone statistics, including changes to entities only. Zone See Changes stays inside the zone.\n- Zone entry failures preserve the previous focus. Zone resizing invalidates old statistics. Overlapping zone outlines handle shared faces correctly.\n\n..."
		},
